before:
Collections.unmodifiableList wraps a live view of the original list. It prevents edits through that view but still reflects edits through the original reference. Neither operation makes arbitrary element objects deeply immutable.

unmodifiableList(source) returns a view that blocks edits through that reference, not through source. In this separate example:
```java
var source = new java.util.ArrayList<>(java.util.List.of("Read"));
var view = java.util.Collections.unmodifiableList(source);
source.add("Write");
System.out.println(view);
```
Output: [Read, Write].

Complete local Java example; the app itself does not execute it:
```java
import java.util.*;
public class Main {

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    var original = new ArrayList<>(List.of("A"));
    var snapshot = List.copyOf(original);
    original.add("B");
    System.out.println(snapshot);
  }
}
```

